# NOTE: this is the OBS variant of the Debian packaging. It intentionally
# differs from the top-level debian/ directory: OBS builds use the bundled
# Rust toolchain (rust-toolchain.tar.zst) instead of system cargo/rustc.
# When changing dependencies here, check whether debian/control needs the
# same change (and vice versa) — there is no automatic sync between them.
Source: rustconn
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Anton Isaiev <totoshko88@gmail.com>
# libwebkitgtk-6.0-dev must be listed here AND in debian.dsc. That is the whole
# lesson of this block; everything below explains why, because the wrong
# explanation has been written here twice already.
#
# For a Debian-style build, OBS builds the chroot from the `Build-Depends` in
# **debian.dsc**, and `dpkg-checkbuilddeps` inside the chroot then validates
# against **this file**. The two are separate lists that nothing keeps in sync.
#
# What that produced:
#
#   * Listed plainly here but absent from debian.dsc — the state in 0.20.10 — the
#     package is never installed, and the build fails at
#     `dpkg-checkbuilddeps: unmet build dependencies: libwebkitgtk-6.0-dev`.
#   * Weakened here to `libwebkitgtk-6.0-dev | libglib2.0-dev`, which is how it was
#     "fixed" then, the check is satisfied by libglib2.0-dev — already present via
#     libgtk-4-dev — so the build succeeds with WebKitGTK still not installed.
#     debian.rules probes with `pkg-config --exists webkitgtk-6.0`, that probe
#     fails, and web-embedded is silently compiled out. Measured across the three
#     deb targets in the 0.21.5 logs: `libwebkitgtk-6.0-dev` zero occurrences,
#     `libglib2.0-dev` two, and the detection line printed `web:` empty. Because
#     the UI is honest about a build without the feature — only System and Custom
#     browser modes appear — the symptom was an option quietly not existing.
#   * Listed in both, which is the state now: OBS installs it, the check passes,
#     the probe succeeds, and the deb gets the browser.
#
# Two explanations previously written here were wrong and are recorded so they are
# not rediscovered as insights. The first said a plain entry is unsatisfiable on
# repositories that do not ship the package — true in general, but not what was
# happening. The second said apt installs the first satisfiable branch of an
# alternative, so each repository opts in or out by itself; OBS does not use apt to
# resolve build dependencies at all, and the branch was never the deciding factor.
#
# All three deb repositories do ship it, checked before the change because a wrong
# answer here breaks three working builds: 2.52.6 on Ubuntu 26.04, 2.52.6-0ubuntu0.24.04.1
# on noble amd64 via universe, 2.52.6-1~deb13u1 on trixie amd64 in main. All are
# above the 2.40 floor `webkit6-sys` 0.6 asks for.
#
# If a repository ever genuinely lacks it, the honest fix is to gate the feature
# for that repository in the OBS project config. Do not weaken this entry to an
# alternative again: that hides the loss instead of reporting it, which is how it
# went unnoticed for two releases.
#
# Runtime linkage needs no entry of its own: `${shlibs:Depends}` derives
# libwebkitgtk-6.0-4 from the binary when, and only when, the feature was compiled
# in.
#
# Comments belong between fields, never inside one: a line starting with '#' in
# the middle of a continuation block ends the field, and everything indented
# after it is a syntax error.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               libgtk-4-dev (>= 4.14),
               libvte-2.91-gtk4-dev (>= 0.76),
               libadwaita-1-dev (>= 1.5),
               libwebkitgtk-6.0-dev,
               libdbus-1-dev,
               libssl-dev,
               libasound2-dev,
               pkg-config,
               zstd,
               gettext
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/totoshko88/RustConn
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: rustconn
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         libgtk-4-1 (>= 4.14),
         libvte-2.91-gtk4-0,
         libadwaita-1-0,
         openssh-client
# libopenh264 is for the external FreeRDP fallback client, which accepts a
# distribution build. The embedded client loads through openh264-sys2, which
# validates the library's SHA-256 against Cisco's own published binaries and
# refuses everything else — see docs/INSTALL.md and RUSTCONN_OPENH264.
# FreeRDP 3 only: RustConn hands FreeRDP its command line through /args-from:,
# which FreeRDP 2 (freerdp2-x11) rejects, so it is never launched (issue #351).
Recommends: freerdp3-x11 | freerdp3-wayland, tigervnc-viewer, virt-viewer, picocom, libopenh264-8 | libopenh264-7
Description: Modern connection manager for SSH, RDP, VNC, SPICE and more
 RustConn is a modern connection manager for Linux with a GTK4/Wayland-native
 interface. Manage SSH, RDP, VNC, SPICE, MOSH, Telnet, Serial, Kubernetes, and
 Zero Trust connections from a single application. Terminals and RDP and VNC
 sessions open inside the window; SPICE sessions open in an external viewer
 (remote-viewer). Some features use other command-line tools, such as a cloud
 provider's CLI for Zero Trust connections or a password manager's client.
 .
 Protocols (embedded Rust implementations):
  - SSH with embedded VTE terminal, split view, and scrollbar
  - RDP via IronRDP (embedded, with FreeRDP fallback)
  - VNC via vnc-rs (embedded, with TigerVNC fallback)
  - SPICE via spice-client (embedded, with remote-viewer fallback)
  - MOSH with predict mode and UDP port range
  - Telnet via external telnet client
  - Serial via picocom (RS-232/USB serial consoles)
  - Kubernetes via kubectl exec (shell access to pods)
  - Zero Trust: AWS SSM, GCP IAP, Azure Bastion, OCI Bastion,
    Cloudflare, Teleport, Tailscale, Boundary, Hoop.dev
 .
 Cloud Sync:
  - Synchronize connections via shared directories (Google Drive,
    Syncthing, Nextcloud, Dropbox, USB)
  - Group Sync with Master/Import access model
  - Simple Sync with UUID-based merge and tombstone deletion
 .
 File Transfer:
  - SFTP file browser via Midnight Commander with split-panel navigation
 .
 Organization:
  - Groups, tags, templates, and smart folders
  - Connection history and statistics
  - Session logging and recording
  - Tab Overview, Tab Pinning, and Tab Switcher
 .
 Import/Export:
  - Asbru-CM, Remmina, SSH config, Ansible inventory, CSV
  - Royal TS, MobaXterm, RDP files, libvirt, native format (.rcn)
 .
 Security:
  - KeePassXC (KDBX files and proxy)
  - System keyring (GNOME Keyring / KDE Wallet)
  - Bitwarden CLI
  - 1Password CLI
  - Passbolt CLI
  - Pass (passwordstore.org)
 .
 Productivity:
  - Split terminals with multi-panel layouts
  - Command snippets and cluster broadcast
  - Custom terminal themes and per-connection color overrides
  - Remote host monitoring (CPU, memory, disk, network)
  - Wake-on-LAN with auto-connect
